  "text": "Camrelizumab-based regimens, including monotherapy and combinations with chemotherapy or targeted therapy, achieve acceptable responses in esophageal cancer patients ([17]; [19]; [18]; [20]; [21]). For example, first-line camrelizumab plus apatinib and chemotherapy achieved ORR and DCR of 80.0% and 96.7% in advanced ESCC patients [21]. The ESCORT-1st study reported an ORR of 72.1% and a DCR of 91.3% with first-line camrelizumab plus chemotherapy in advanced or metastatic ESCC patients [18]. The CAP 02 Re-challenge study reported ORR and DCR of 10.2% and 69.4% in advanced ESCC patients with prior immune checkpoint inhibitors [20]. Overall, previous studies reported ORR of 10.2%–80.0% and DCR of 69.4%–96.7% in advanced esophageal cancer patients receiving camrelizumab-based regimens ([17]; [19]; [18]; [20]; [21]). In this study, ORR and DCR were 18.8% and 84.4%, respectively, within previously reported ranges ([17]; [19]; [18]; [20]; [21]), suggesting satisfactory real-world efficacy. Previous real-world studies reported ORR and DCR of 15.1% to 72.0% and 35.8%–92.0% with PD-1 inhibitor-based regimens ([22]; [23]; [24]), indicating comparable efficacy of camrelizumab to other PD-1 inhibitors and supporting its use as a potential immunotherapy option for advanced esophageal cancer."
}

